{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact38550a34b954b07fa862dd87a412bc3ac93dfd0c. Sole primary858acf064382d4a0272577eb12d624152c448b51 read first/full; all230lines reviewed. Draft2 substantially answers the prior findings: ordinary merge preserves the destination vocabulary; grants/local service and a conditional destination probe are explicit; assessments are separate from approval; retry count moves to send-back; native clock freshness and source-chosen expiry limits are honest. Three remaining design gaps prevent approval.\n\nF1 — High, sections3.2/3.4 and witnesses: the new approval branch is not the claimed fence. Its anyOf accepts any merger with one locally counted approve review, including the designated lander with missing/untrusted/below-bar assessment. That contradicts \"the lander's merge passes only by the rule\" and its negative witness. More seriously, setting destination approvals to0 means this extra local count does not inherit the destination's approval eligibility checks. For example, a controller can leave a local submitted approval for its agent's version while ownerMayReview=false; the destination excludes it as independent but still meets its zero approval requirement. A compromised review key is likewise excluded from the destination's counted approvals without making zero unmet (reservation.ts:233–236,269–274). Thus \"ownerMayReview still applies\" does not establish the required one eligible person's approval on the fallback route. Make the lander versus human-authorized routes explicit and enforce the fallback's actual eligible approval at the authority boundary, or name the required future versioned support. Keep old pins unchanged. Witness both the lander-with-person-approval case and a non-lander with only an ineligible approval; neither may accidentally bypass the chosen policy.\n\nF2 — Medium, section3.2: name a feasible bounded input path for assessment and ownTests. Current merge already binds proposal, manifest and rules. Binding assessment uses the fourth also subject; binding its passed ownTests job would require a fifth, rejected by the present bound (contract/definition.ts:86–88; bounds.ts:88; derive/validate/handlers.ts:61). The rule cannot dereference an arbitrary local job ID. State a route within the existing allowance, such as native job guards/authenticated fact inputs with explicit current-version/tree/name/configuration binding, or reclassify the dependency. Preserve the old merge fields the destination needs. Also record the assessor as a signer-bound party of the assessment item: the current outline lists values and manifest ref but no stored assessor, while auto-landing and uniqueness depend on it (contract/read.ts:41–58 has no implicit creator member on an item). An assessor identity supplied as ordinary model data is insufficient. This needs an architectural description, not production JSONata.\n\nF3 — Medium, section3.3: finish the recorded outcome protocol. \"If merge is refused, escalate with the refusal's reason\" cannot fit the four allowed escalation reasons: existing refusals include approvals-needed, rules-unknown and merge-in-progress. Define a bounded mapping rather than passing arbitrary refusal text. More importantly, accepted merge opens an intended item; committed and unknown remain nonfinal, and only destination outcome published marks the proposal merged (change.ts:190–193,1616–1645). Keep pending/unknown publication explicit and observe/recover the existing operation without blindly resubmitting it; do not call an accepted merge a recorded landing or promise every version already ends in one of three terminal outcomes. A failed/aborted publication needs its defined send-back/escalation handling. Add a lost-reply/pending witness and an ordinary merge-refusal mapping witness.\n\nArchitecture: the revised direction is coherent as conditional data plus local service, with the destination probe before implementation assumptions. The remaining issues are enforcement of the fallback approval, bounded rule inputs and the native publication lifecycle.\n\nSecurity: advisory confidence remains distinct from counted review authority; F1 must preserve independence and key-compromise policy even when automatic extents request zero approvals. F4 from draft1 is closed at design level: native before plus presence, with host-stamped/bounded freshness explicitly future.\n\nSimplification: retain one merge protocol and a small process. A short bounded-input description and a closed outcome table are enough; no generic coordinator is needed.\n\nValidation: compared complete candidate to Mainaeebc18c: sole authored delta is this230-line note; diff --check passes. Source citations and native boundaries above checked. MVP link is valid at its current root path; evidence-rules remains a separately reviewed, unlanded dependency. No source/index changes, package installs, provider calls, deployments or repeated whole gate for prose.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:858acf064382d4a0272577eb12d624152c448b51","binding":"assigned","head":"38550a34b954b07fa862dd87a412bc3ac93dfd0c","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:61b4bb8099d0b7b6b3ec87d59fe4d1aafe4407f0\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:64ce9be2beda6cbb28e09aa9de06be70e68a797d\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:448d93060e5e8461eac18d5200e8835e1c0785e5","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}